    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> kingdom ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty">Nyiginya dynasty</a>, whose sacred mwami governed through army, land and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> chiefs.</li><li>It reached its greatest extent and centralisation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri">Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</a> in the late nineteenth century.</li><li>German and Belgian rule hardened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi into fixed categories; the monarchy was abolished in 1961.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Rwanda had no fixed capital for most of its history: the royal court moved between residences, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda">Nyanza</a> became the permanent seat only around 1899, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a> began as a German residency in 1907.</p></aside>
    </section>

