    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagera-river.jpg" alt="Kagera River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Kagera River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KageraRuvubu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SteveRwanda aka Amakuru, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Rwanda took shape in the hills east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-kivu">Lake Kivu</a>, among farming and herding communities that shared a single language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinyarwanda">Kinyarwanda</a>, and clans that cut across the groups later classed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>, Tutsi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes-twa">Twa</a>. Court traditions, recorded in the twentieth century by the priest and scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexis-kagame">Alexis Kagame</a>, trace a long line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty">Nyiginya</a> kings back many centuries, with an early core near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-muhazi">Lake Muhazi</a>. Modern historians, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-vansina">Jan Vansina</a>, treat the earliest reigns as legendary and see the effective founder of the kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruganzu-ii-ndori">Ruganzu II Ndori</a>, who probably seized power in the mid-seventeenth century and is remembered as the restorer of a realm shattered by foreign invasions. From a base in central Rwanda the Nyiginya kings expanded by war, alliance and the absorption of neighbouring polities. The eighteenth-century king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyirima-ii-rujugira">Cyirima II Rujugira</a> is credited by tradition with stationing permanent armies on the frontiers facing the Kingdom of Burundi, Gisaka and Ndorwa. Around the end of the eighteenth century Rwanda and Burundi partitioned the Kingdom of Bugesera; at about the same time Rwandan armies overran Ndorwa in the north-east; and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutara-ii-rwogera">Mutara II Rwogera</a> Rwanda annexed Gisaka around the 1850s, creating a compact, densely settled realm between Lake Kivu and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagera-river">Akagera River</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rwanda's economy rested on intensive hoe farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, beans and bananas, later joined by American crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, combined with the herding of long-horned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> that carried great social prestige. The categories <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi were originally linked to occupation, wealth and closeness to power rather than to fixed descent, but they became steadily more political as the kingdom grew. Cattle clientage, <em>ubuhake</em>, bound a client to a patron who granted the use of cows in return for services and loyalty, and in the late nineteenth century a heavier labour obligation, <em>uburetwa</em>, required many Hutu farmers to work for chiefs. The historian Catharine Newbury has shown how these institutions hardened social hierarchy in the decades before and during colonial rule. Long-distance trade was limited compared with Buganda or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> coast; local markets dealt in hoes, salt, pottery, livestock and produce, and the court largely kept Arab and Swahili caravans out of the country. Religion centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imana">Imana</a>, the creator, on ancestral spirits and on the initiatory cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryangombe">Ryangombe</a>, known as <em>kubandwa</em>, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> itself. Catholic missions of the White Fathers, founded from 1900, converted much of the population during the colonial period, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> had become the majority religion by the mid-twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri.jpg" alt="Kigeli IV Rwabugiri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri">Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mwami_Kigeri_IV_Rwabugiri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Military organisation lay at the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty">Nyiginya</a> state. Every lineage was attached to a named social army, <em>umutwe</em>, which a king created at the start of his reign; young men of the elite trained at court as <em>intore</em>, learning warfare together with dance, poetry and eloquence, while each army's herds and dependants formed a corporation that outlived the king who founded it. Armies fought on foot with spears and bows; horses played no part, and firearms were almost unknown before the colonial period. The royal drum <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a>, hung with trophies taken from defeated enemy rulers, symbolised victorious kingship. Expansion came through repeated campaigns, often spread over generations, against Burundi, Gisaka, Ndorwa and the small states of the north and west. The greatest warrior king was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri">Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</a>, who fought almost every year, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijwi-island">Ijwi Island</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-kivu">Lake Kivu</a>, raided Bushi to the west and Nkore to the north-east, and died in 1895 while on campaign. In 1896 Rwandan forces attacked a post of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shangi">Shangi</a> in the south-west and were driven back with heavy losses by rifle fire, a demonstration of the technological gap that would soon make armed resistance to European rule hopeless.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga.jpg" alt="Yuhi V Musinga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga">Yuhi V Musinga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuhiVMusinga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <em>mwami</em> was a sacred king, believed to embody the welfare of the land, and ruled alongside the queen mother, who held considerable power of her own. Hereditary ritual specialists, the <em>abiru</em>, guarded the esoteric code of kingship, <em>ubwiru</em>, which prescribed royal rituals and regulated the succession, and kings bore names drawn from a fixed cycle that included Mutara, Kigeli, Mibambwe and Yuhi. There was no fixed capital: the court moved between royal residences, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda">Nyanza</a> became the permanent seat only under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga">Yuhi V Musinga</a> around 1899. Nineteenth-century provincial government combined army chiefs with separate chiefs for land and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, whose overlapping jurisdictions allowed the king to play officials against one another. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri">Kigeli IV Rwabugiri</a> strengthened the centre by appointing favourites in place of entrenched lineages and by extending royal administration to peripheral regions that had kept their own rulers. Succession remained fragile: in 1896, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-rucunshu">Coup of Rucunshu</a>, his chosen heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mibambwe-iv-rutarindwa">Mibambwe IV Rutarindwa</a> was overthrown and killed by the faction of the queen mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanjogera">Kanjogera</a>, which placed her son Musinga on the throne. Under Belgian rule the triple chieftaincy was merged into a single chief in reforms of 1926–1931, almost all posts went to Tutsi, and identity cards recording each person's ethnic category were introduced in the early 1930s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda.jpg" alt="Nyanza, Rwanda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda">Nyanza, Rwanda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King%27s_palace_in_Nyanza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guswen, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rwanda was one of the most centralised and densely populated states of pre-colonial eastern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and its court preserved one of the richest oral literatures of the region. The dynastic poems, <em>ibisigo</em>, and historical narratives kept by court specialists were collected in the twentieth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexis-kagame">Alexis Kagame</a> and later analysed critically by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-vansina">Jan Vansina</a>, making the kingdom a classic case in the study of African oral tradition. Its nineteenth-century conquests largely defined the territory of the modern country, and its language, dances and royal symbols became central to Rwandan national identity. Its most consequential legacy, however, lay in the way Europeans interpreted it. German and Belgian administrators and missionaries, influenced by the so-called Hamitic hypothesis, treated the Tutsi as a foreign ruling race and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> as their natural subjects, and governed through a Tutsi aristocracy. Colonial reforms turned the fluid social distinctions of the kingdom into fixed, officially recorded ethnic identities. This racialised reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty">Nyiginya</a> kingdom fed the Hutu revolution of 1959–1962, the flight of perhaps 200,000 Tutsi refugees to neighbouring countries and the cycles of violence that culminated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against the Tutsi in 1994. The royal palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyanza-rwanda">Nyanza</a> is today preserved as a museum.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gustav-adolf-von-gotzen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-adolf-von-gotzen.jpg" alt="Gustav Adolf von Götzen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gustav-adolf-von-gotzen">Gustav Adolf von Götzen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustav_Adolf_von_G%C3%B6tzen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom's independence ended within a few years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-iv-rwabugiri">Rwabugiri</a>'s death. The German officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-adolf-von-gotzen">Gustav Adolf von Götzen</a> crossed Rwanda in 1894, and in 1897 the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuhi-v-musinga">Yuhi V Musinga</a>, weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-rucunshu">Rucunshu coup</a> and the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shangi">Shangi</a>, accepted German protection, making Rwanda part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>. The Germans ruled indirectly, founding a residency at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a> in 1907 and helping the king subdue the autonomous north. In 1916 Belgian troops from the Congo occupied the country, and from 1922 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> governed it as part of Ruanda-Urundi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory. Belgium deposed Musinga in 1931 for resisting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and installed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutara-iii-rudahigwa">Mutara III Rudahigwa</a>, who was baptised in 1943 and abolished <em>ubuhake</em> in 1954. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu-manifesto">Hutu Manifesto</a> of 1957 demanded an end to the Tutsi monopoly of power. After Mutara III died suddenly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Usumbura</a> in July 1959, his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigeli-v-ndahindurwa">Kigeli V Ndahindurwa</a> succeeded amid rising tension, and violence in November 1959 began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> revolution, which Belgian authorities supported. On 28 January 1961 Hutu leaders meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhanga">Gitarama</a> proclaimed a republic, and a referendum supervised by the United Nations in September 1961 confirmed the abolition of the monarchy. Rwanda became independent as a republic on 1 July 1962.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Rwanda built one of the most centralised states of pre-colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> through sacred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyiginya-dynasty">Nyiginya</a> kingship, social armies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> clientage, and the colonial reading of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>–Tutsi hierarchy as racial helped produce the revolution of 1959, the end of the monarchy in 1961 and the violent politics of modern Rwanda.</em></p>

